#
# What turns the inject Envoy on (ADR-0040): the one value that differs from env.example, as an env
# file that goes IN FRONT of it (image/gateway/render takes the first definition of a key, so a
# file listed earlier wins). A deployment that enables envoy-inject.service writes this line
# into /etc/mediatore-gateway/env, above the rest, or lists this file first. Whole-line comments only;
# no '|' in a value. Nothing here is a secret.
#
# The data plane's side needs nothing from this file: it asks the authorizer's CHECK socket by
# default (env.example), with or without inject, and the host table carries no loopback exception.
# What inject adds at the authorizer is a second socket, INJECT_AUTHORIZER_SOCKET, in a directory of
# its own: the CHECK and INJECT sockets never share one, since the directory is what carries each
# Envoy's access (ADR-0040 decision 6). Inject is not for a gateway that keeps the check on loopback
# TCP: with a credential in play, any local process could then ask the authorizer a question.
#
# The host table admits the inject port to the sandbox range under the relay's per-source cap
# (ADR-0040 decision 7): the element is ", <INJECT_PORT>", appended to the relay's ports.
INJECT_PORT_ELEMENT=, 3133
